mosdns-router (0.3.2) unstable; urgency=high

  * The install no longer refuses a machine this package provisions. preflight
    demanded a default ACL from every state directory, and /run/mosdns is
    deliberately provisioned at 2750 with no ACL at all, so the install refused and
    dpkg left the package half-configured -- on every release, since the change that
    made /run/mosdns 2750 shipped in 0.2.0. Found by the Podman integration matrix,
    which had been blocked until now, failing on 22.04, 24.04 and 26.04 at once.
  * The default ACL's requirement is now two questions rather than one. Where the
    group can write the directory it must still grant the group at least what the
    directory needs, because an unprivileged creator exists there. Where the group
    cannot, no default ACL is required -- but one that is WIDER than the directory is
    still refused: `default:group::rwx` on a 2750 directory makes a root-created file
    group-writable, and rewriting a file in place needs no permission in the directory.
  * An ACL granting more than the requirement is no longer refused for not matching
    it exactly, and the two refusals now say which half of the ACL is at fault and
    whether it is missing or too wide. The old wording read "its default ACL does not
    grant the group 'r-x'" while listing, two words later, an ACL that grants exactly
    that, and printed the whole getfacl output as the default half's contents when it
    was printing the access half.

 -- mosdns-router maintainers <mosdns-router@localhost>  Sun, 05 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000

mosdns-router (0.3.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Closing the response rewriter's ECH provider now WAITS for the background
    refresher it stops, instead of signalling it and returning. A tick in flight
    writes the state document, so a Close that returned early was a Close that said
    "you may move this directory" to a goroutine about to write into it. The wait is
    bounded by one fetch budget, because a tick carries its own timeout, so Close
    cannot hang. Found as a CI test-teardown flake -- the testing framework removed a
    temporary directory the refresher was still writing into -- and fixed as the
    production defect it is rather than as the flake it showed up as.

 -- mosdns-router maintainers <mosdns-router@localhost>  Sun, 05 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000

mosdns-router (0.3.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * policy.yaml's `schedule` now changes when the daily list work runs. It was
    validated as HH:MM and read by nothing, while the timer that actually decided
    carried a hardcoded OnCalendar, so an operator who changed the policy got no
    change at all. The timer is now rendered from the policy and systemd is
    reloaded as part of writing it. The default moves from 03:00 to 03:30, which is
    the time the hardcoded timer was already using, so making the field real did not
    also move the machine half an hour earlier.
  * Two new switches, both off: lists.china.automatic and lists.cloudflare.automatic.
    They are separate because the risks differ -- Cloudflare's ranges choose which
    CDN edge an answer is rewritten to, data/cn decides which names take the
    foreign branch at all, so bundling them would mean opening the cheap one opens
    the expensive one. The China list's refresh refuses a list that has lost more
    than 5% of its rules, and leaves everything published untouched when it does.
  * `update-lists --pin-remote` now takes an optional commit, and archives the
    outgoing pin to source-lock.previous.json before publishing. Until now a pin
    accepted by mistake could not be undone, because the command could only name
    upstream's current HEAD. A publication that cannot be archived now stops rather
    than proceeding without a rollback target.
  * `mosdns-cdnctl render` publishes the generated timer into
    /usr/lib/systemd/system and reloads systemd. It takes --unit-out for the
    directory, which is separate from --out because systemd does not read units from
    the document directory.
  * The daily list work is no longer a report-only unit: it runs the new
    `--automatic` mode, so it may publish. Its sandbox is sized for that --
    ReadWritePaths for the two state directories, a umask, and exit 4 excused,
    because publishing takes the control lock and a daily job that skipped because
    the router was being reconfigured is not a failed day.

 -- mosdns-router maintainers <mosdns-router@localhost>  Sun, 05 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000

mosdns-router (0.2.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * No change to the shipped program. The package contents are identical to
    0.2.0; what moved is how a Release is produced.
  * Publishing is now a separate, explicit workflow that takes the ID of a build
    run and promotes THAT artifact. build-deb.yml used to build and publish in one
    run, so a Release was whatever the last run produced and nothing was ever
    selected. There is still no path to a Release that is not a run in the Actions
    list.
  * The release tag must now equal the package version. A release tagged v0.2.1
    carrying a package whose control field says 0.2.0 was possible and was a trap
    for whoever installed it.
  * The artifact assertions moved into scripts/assert-deb.sh so the build and the
    publish step run the same ones, rather than two copies that would drift.

 -- mosdns-router maintainers <mosdns-router@localhost>  Sun, 05 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000

mosdns-router (0.2.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The foreign route is configurable. policy.yaml gains `foreign.upstreams`, a
    list of the packaged DNSCrypt resolver and/or mosdns-dialed upstreams in any
    scheme mosdns accepts, plus `foreign.concurrent` (default 2) and a
    `foreign_cache` block carrying the cache size and an optional record-time
    bound. The routing document derives its forward list from it, and the ECH key
    is fetched over TCP through whichever enabled entry can carry it -- a policy
    with no such entry is refused by name rather than rendered.
  * NEW VERB: `mosdns-cdnctl check-upstream` reports whether each enabled entry
    can be dialled and can answer, and `mosdns-cdnctl flush-cache` empties the
    router's foreign answer cache through the cache plugin's own endpoint.
  * BREAKING, and it is the kind an operator has to act on: a policy.yaml that
    predates `foreign.upstreams` no longer validates, and the refusal says what
    to add. policy.yaml is a conffile, so a customised copy survives the upgrade
    and the router will not start against it until `foreign.upstreams` is added --
    the two lines the shipped policy carries restore the previous behaviour.
  * The routing document gains an `api` block on 127.0.0.1:15354, which is what
    makes the cache plugin's own flush endpoint reachable. It is loopback and
    unprivileged, like every other listener this package configures.

 -- mosdns-router maintainers <mosdns-router@localhost>  Sun, 04 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000

mosdns-router (0.1.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * First packaged release: the DNS router, the DNSCrypt foreign resolver, the
    CDN optimizer and its timers, the DHCP bridge hook, and the transactional
    installer that points this machine's NetworkManager connection at 127.0.0.1.

